    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you give me a breakdown of the different ways legal scholars approach interpreting the Second Amendment? Specifically, I'm looking to understand the tension between originalist methods and living constitutionalism in that context.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Legal scholars generally divide their approach to the Second Amendment into two major camps: originalism, which seeks to determine the meaning of the text (specifically \"the right of the people to secure a well regulated Militia\") at the time of its 1791 adoption, and living constitutionalism, which argues that the amendment must be interpreted through the lens of modern conceptions of individual liberty and self-defense.\n\nThe primary tension lies in how each theory views the scope of \"arms available\" and the government's \"unreasonable regulation.\" Originalists often hail from the 2008 *District of Columbia v. Heller* decision to affirm a collective right tied to militia service via the 18th and 19th Amendments, whereas living constitutionalists emphasize that the right is always individual, as the \"right of the people\" was in use in the 18th century and remains so today.\n\nThis debate ultimately centers on whether the Bill of Rights should be applied rigidly based on 18th-century understandings or if it must evolve alongside contemporary societal values and government capabilities.",
      "source": "target",
